## 1.1. Mixed-Matrix Membranes
Mixed-matrix membranes (MMMs) are composite membranes made by combining an inorganic or inorganic-organic hybrid material in the form of micro- or nanoparticles (the discrete or dispersed phase; additive or filler) and a polymer matrix (the continuous phase) (Scheme 1).[16] By using two materials with different transport properties, such membranes have the potential to combine synergistically the easy processability of polymers and the superior gas separation performance of porous filler materials. MMMs may provide separation properties which can be above the Robeson upper bound by overcoming the trade-off between selectivity and permeability which is typical for pure polymer membranes.
